<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anatolia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Anatolian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anatolia, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, is the large peninsula forming most of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. It was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> and was later ruled or contested by the Achaemenid, Seleucid and Byzantine empires. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> in 1071 Turkish groups settled it under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, and it became the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anatolia.jpg" alt="Anatolia" width="320" height="150" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Golden · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_geographic_region_of_Anatolia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
